1. **A computer-implemented method for detecting use of devices at a property, the method comprising:**

receiving, by one or more processors, utility consumption data generated by a utility meter associated with the property, the utility consumption data comprising a time series of aggregate utility-consumption measurements for a plurality of devices at the property;

detecting variations in the aggregate utility-consumption measurements based on changes between measurements within respective time intervals;

identifying, from the detected variations, candidate on-events corresponding to increases in utility consumption and candidate off-events corresponding to decreases in utility consumption;

matching respective candidate on-events with respective candidate off-events based at least in part on a correspondence between magnitudes of the increases and decreases and a temporal relationship between the candidate on-events and the candidate off-events, thereby generating matched event pairs;

grouping the matched event pairs into event groups based on at least one of temporal proximity, consumption magnitude, event duration, or a recurring usage pattern;

clustering the event groups, based on respective utility-consumption signatures of the event groups, into a plurality of clusters associated with respective devices or device types at the property; and

determining that a specific device at the property was used during a time period based on at least one event group occurring during the time period being assigned to a cluster having a utility-consumption signature corresponding to the specific device.

### Computer-Implemented Method for Detecting Device Usage from Utility-Meter Readings

In one aspect, a computer-implemented method is provided for determining an occurrence or an absence of usage of one or more devices of interest at a property. The method may be performed by one or more processors in communication with one or more utility meters associated with the property.

The method comprises receiving, from the one or more utility meters, a time-ordered series of utility-consumption readings. The utility-consumption readings may represent consumption of electricity, natural gas, water, thermal energy, or another metered utility. Each reading may include a consumption value, a demand value, a cumulative meter value, a flow rate, or a combination thereof, together with a corresponding timestamp.

The received readings are normalized to produce an interval-consumption series. Normalization may include validating timestamps, removing duplicate readings, estimating missing readings, converting cumulative meter values into interval-consumption values, resampling the readings to a selected temporal resolution, correcting for meter resets, and adjusting for differences in meter scale or reporting frequency.

A plurality of analysis intervals are defined within the interval-consumption series. For each analysis interval, the one or more processors generate a feature vector characterizing utility consumption during or proximate to that interval. The feature vector may include one or more of:

1. total utility consumption;
2. average, minimum, or maximum consumption;
3. peak demand or flow rate;
4. a change in consumption relative to a preceding interval;
5. a duration of elevated consumption;
6. a rate of increase or decrease;
7. a number or magnitude of detected state transitions;
8. time-of-day, day-of-week, or season information;
9. spectral, waveform, or load-shape characteristics;
10. consumption measured by multiple respective utility meters; and
11. a difference between measured consumption and an estimated baseline.

The generated feature vectors are processed using a clustering procedure to form a plurality of clusters. The clustering procedure may comprise, for example, k-means clustering, hierarchical clustering, density-based clustering, Gaussian-mixture modeling, spectral clustering, or another supervised, semi-supervised, or unsupervised clustering technique. Each cluster represents a recurring utility-consumption pattern observed at the property or across a plurality of properties.

At least one of the clusters is associated with usage of a device of interest. The association may be established using labeled training intervals, a known device signature, commissioning data, user-provided device information, controlled activation or deactivation of the device, correlation with an auxiliary sensor, or statistical comparison with consumption patterns from properties known to include the device. A further cluster may be associated with non-usage of the device, background consumption, or usage of another device.

For a target analysis interval, the one or more processors determine a membership measure between the feature vector for the target analysis interval and one or more of the clusters. The membership measure may comprise a distance from a cluster centroid, a probability under a cluster distribution, a density estimate, a cluster-assignment score, or a combination thereof.

An occurrence of usage of the device of interest is determined when the feature vector for the target analysis interval is assigned to, or satisfies a threshold relationship with, a cluster associated with device usage. An absence of usage is determined when the feature vector is assigned to a cluster associated with non-usage or when the membership measure for each device-usage cluster fails to satisfy a usage threshold. In some implementations, an indeterminate classification is generated when the membership measures do not satisfy either a usage threshold or a non-usage threshold.

The determination may further be based on temporal constraints corresponding to operation of the device of interest. Such constraints may include a minimum operating duration, a maximum operating duration, a required sequence of start and stop transitions, a minimum time between activations, an expected duty cycle, or consistency across multiple consecutive analysis intervals. Cluster assignments for adjacent intervals may accordingly be combined into a device-usage event having a start time, an end time, a duration, and an estimated amount of utility consumed by the device.

A confidence value may be generated for the determined occurrence or absence of usage. The confidence value may be based on distance to a cluster centroid, separation from competing clusters, consistency with the temporal constraints, data completeness, meter resolution, or agreement among readings from multiple utility meters.

The one or more processors store or output a device-status record indicating at least:

- an identifier of the property;
- an identifier or type of the device of interest;
- a time period to which the determination applies;
- an indication of occurrence, absence, or indeterminate usage; and
- optionally, the confidence value or estimated device-specific consumption.

The device-status record may be used to generate a notification, update a property-management interface, identify an anomalous operating condition, verify performance of installed equipment, estimate resource savings, determine compliance with an operating requirement, or control another device at the property.

In certain embodiments, separate clusters are generated for respective properties, thereby accounting for property-specific background loads. In other embodiments, clusters are generated from readings collected across multiple properties and are subsequently adapted to a particular property. The clusters may be updated periodically or incrementally in response to newly received readings, confirmed classifications, seasonal changes, changes in property occupancy, or changes in the devices installed at the property.

### Exemplary Claim-Style Formulation

A computer-implemented method comprising:

1. receiving, by one or more processors, time-stamped utility-meter readings associated with a property;
2. deriving, from the utility-meter readings, interval-consumption data for a plurality of time intervals;
3. generating, for respective time intervals, feature vectors representing utility-consumption characteristics;
4. applying a clustering procedure to the feature vectors to identify a plurality of clusters representing recurring consumption patterns;
5. associating at least a first cluster of the plurality of clusters with usage of a device of interest;
6. comparing a feature vector for a target time interval with the plurality of clusters;
7. determining, based at least in part on a cluster membership measure resulting from the comparison, whether usage of the device of interest occurred or was absent during the target time interval; and
8. causing a device-status record representing the determined occurrence or absence of usage to be stored, transmitted, or displayed.

1. **A computer-implemented method for disaggregating appliance consumption from aggregate consumption data, the method comprising:**

receiving, by one or more processors, a time series of aggregate consumption measurements representing combined consumption by a plurality of devices;

detecting variations in the aggregate consumption measurements;

designating positive variations as activation events and negative variations as deactivation events;

matching respective activation events with respective deactivation events based on at least one matching criterion;

grouping one or more matched pairs of activation and deactivation events into respective blocks, each block representing a candidate period of device use;

determining, for each block, a feature representation based on the events included in the block and a portion of the aggregate consumption measurements associated with the block;

comparing the feature representation of each block with predefined clusters corresponding to respective devices of interest;

classifying a block as use of a particular device of interest based on the feature representation satisfying a similarity criterion for a predefined cluster corresponding to the particular device; and

generating disaggregated consumption data for the particular device from the block classified as use of the particular device.
